(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-phases-of-sleep-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Phases of Sleep Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What happens to muscle tone during rapid eye movement sleep?","choices":["It rises above waking tone","It matches the awake state","It is lost mainly in the diaphragm","It is very depressed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Muscle tone is very depressed during rapid eye movement sleep, which indicates inhibition of the spinal muscle control areas. The muscle recording shows atonia of voluntary muscles, sparing the eye muscles and the diaphragm. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 10, 28"},{"q":"What determines the intensity and pattern of electrical activity on the electroencephalogram?","choices":["How regular the heartbeat is","How deep the muscle atonia is","How fast the eyes are moving","How excited brain regions are"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The intensity and patterns of electrical activity on the electroencephalogram are determined by the level of excitation of different parts of the brain, which varies with sleep, wakefulness and brain disorders such as epilepsy or psychosis. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 10, 21, 28"},{"q":"How well understood are the specific physiological functions of sleep?","choices":["Still a mystery under study","Fully explained by energy savings","Limited to the nervous system","Unknown, and sleep is optional"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sleep has important physiological functions, and restricting it harms performance and health, but researchers have limited understanding of why sleep is essential; its specific functions remain a mystery and continue to be researched. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 17"},{"q":"With an average of 8 hours of sleep and cycles of 90 to 120 minutes, how many sleep cycles occur in a night?","choices":["1 to 2 cycles","2 to 3 cycles","4 to 5 cycles","8 to 10 cycles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An average night has about 8 hours of sleep containing 4 to 5 separate sleep cycles, each lasting 90 to 120 minutes; 8 hours divided by cycles of that length gives 4 to 5, each holding non-rapid eye movement stages and a rapid eye movement episode. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 9"},{"q":"Which wave characterizes alert wakefulness?","choices":["Delta waves","Beta waves","Alpha waves","Theta waves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Alert wakefulness is characterized by high-frequency beta waves: the person is awake, alert and active with the eyes open, actively processing information. Relaxing with the eyes closed shifts the recording to alpha waves. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 23, 25, 26, 27, 28"},{"q":"Which proposed function of sleep helps a person forget unimportant information?","choices":["Targeted erasure of synapses","Neural maturation","Clearance of metabolic waste","Conservation of metabolic energy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Targeted erasure of synapses is one of the functions of sleep: it lets the brain forget unimportant information that might otherwise clutter the synaptic network. It sits beside neural maturation, learning, cognition, waste clearance and energy conservation. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which function of sleep removes products generated by neural activity in the awake brain?","choices":["Body and tissue repair","Neural maturation","Clearance of metabolic waste","Conservation of metabolic energy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Clearance of metabolic waste products generated by neural activity in the awake brain is one of the functions of sleep, alongside neural maturation, facilitation of learning, targeted synapse erasure, cognition and conservation of metabolic energy. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 17, 28"},{"q":"Despite brain waves that resemble wakefulness, what is the state of a person in rapid eye movement sleep?","choices":["Awake and relaxed with eyes closed","Asleep, not fully aware of surroundings","Awake but unable to move the limbs","Unarousable by stimuli, as in coma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In rapid eye movement sleep the brain is very active and its waves resemble wakefulness, yet the person is asleep and not fully aware of the surroundings. That mismatch is why the state is also called paradoxical sleep. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 5, 9, 10, 28"},{"q":"In the proposed theory, what ends a long period of wakefulness?","choices":["Fatigue of activating neurons","Stronger positive feedback","Release of activating nuclei","Rising parasympathetic tone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. After the brain has remained active for many hours, neurons in the activating system become fatigued. The positive feedback cycle between the mesencephalic reticular nuclei and the cerebral cortex fades, and the sleep-promoting centers take over. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 14, 18, 19"},{"q":"Which outcome follows sleep restriction over a few days?","choices":["Longer rapid eye movement episodes","Worse cognitive and physical performance","Higher overall productivity","Faster learning and memory"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sleep restriction over a few days can decrease a person's cognitive and physical performance, overall productivity and health. That is one reason sleep is considered essential, even though its specific physiological functions are still being researched. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 9, 16, 17"},{"q":"How long does each sleep cycle last?","choices":["5 to 30 minutes","40 to 60 minutes","90 to 120 minutes","180 to 240 minutes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Each sleep cycle lasts 90 to 120 minutes, and an average 8 hours of sleep contains 4 to 5 of them, each running from non-rapid eye movement stages into a rapid eye movement episode. That is why rapid eye movement episodes recur about every 90 minutes. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 7, 9"},{"q":"In the proposed sleep-wakefulness theory, what happens when the sleep centers are not activated?","choices":["Activating neurons become fatigued","Sleep-promoting centers take over","The cortex stops exciting the nuclei","Activating nuclei lose their inhibition"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When the sleep centers are not activated, the mesencephalic and upper pontine reticular activating nuclei are released from inhibition. They then activate spontaneously, excite the cerebral cortex and peripheral nervous system, and receive positive feedback that keeps them active. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 18, 19"},{"q":"Which wave does the electroencephalogram show during rapid eye movement sleep?","choices":["Delta waves","Beta waves","Alpha waves","Theta waves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rapid eye movement sleep shows beta waves, irregular and high in frequency, similar to the awake state but accompanied by atonia. That is why it is often difficult to tell rapid eye movement sleep from alert wakefulness on the electroencephalogram. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 26, 27, 28, 29"},{"q":"Which electroencephalogram change marks the first, light stage of non-rapid eye movement sleep?","choices":["Sawtooth waves appear","Beta waves dominate","Voltage becomes low","Voltage rises 2 to 4 times"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In the first stage of non-rapid eye movement sleep, the stage of light sleep, the voltage of the electroencephalogram becomes low. Frequency then slows progressively as sleep deepens toward delta waves. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 23, 26, 27, 28"},{"q":"In an awake, relaxed person, low-voltage waves on the electroencephalogram give way to rhythmical alpha waves. What change best explains this?","choices":["The person entered deep sleep","The eyes were closed","The eyes were opened","Attention turned to a mental task"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In an awake person, opening the eyes replaces the alpha rhythm with low-voltage activity, and closing them brings alpha waves back. The awake, eyes-closed state of relaxation and decreased alertness is characterized by alpha waves. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 22, 24, 26, 28"},{"q":"Which recording shows the irregular, sharply peaked eye movements of rapid eye movement sleep?","choices":["Electromyogram","Electroencephalogram","Blood pressure recording","Electrooculogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The electrooculogram, the eye-movement recording, shows the irregular, sharply peaked eye movements of rapid eye movement sleep. The eye muscles are spared from the atonia that affects other voluntary muscles, so the eyes keep moving. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 10, 12, 28"},{"q":"Why are dreams during non-rapid eye movement sleep often not remembered?","choices":["The brain is in a coma-like state","They are not consolidated in memory","No dreams occur in this sleep","They are too vivid to store"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dreams during non-rapid eye movement sleep are often not remembered because the dreams are not consolidated in memory. Dreams and nightmares do occur in this sleep, which is why calling it the dreamless state is not accurate; they are simply less vivid and usually lost. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 5, 9, 13, 14"},{"q":"After many hours of activity, activating-system neurons fatigue and their feedback loop with the cortex fades. What happens next?","choices":["The activating nuclei are released","Unarousable coma sets in","Rapid eye movement sleep starts at once","Sleep-promoting centers take over"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With the activating neurons fatigued and the positive feedback between the mesencephalic reticular nuclei and the cerebral cortex fading, the sleep-promoting centers take over and the brain makes the transition from wakefulness to sleep. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 5, 7, 9, 19"},{"q":"Which stage shows a falling heart rate and temperature and is the longest stage by total duration?","choices":["Stage N2","Stage N1","Stage N3","Rapid eye movement sleep"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Stage N2 is less responsive, deeper sleep in which heart rate and temperature decrease, and it is the longest stage by total duration, making up the majority of the sleep cycle. Its electroencephalogram shows theta waves. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 7, 9, 10, 13, 28"},{"q":"What do sleep and coma have in common?","choices":["Spontaneous morning waking","Cycles of 90 to 120 minutes","Unconsciousness","Arousal by sensory stimuli"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sleep and coma are both states of unconsciousness. They differ in arousability: a sleeping person can be aroused by sensory or other stimuli, whereas a person in coma cannot be aroused by stimuli. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 5, 6, 10"},{"q":"At what frequency do delta waves occur?","choices":["Less than 3.5 cycles per second","4 to 7 cycles per second","8 to 13 cycles per second","Greater than 14 cycles per second"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Delta waves include all waves with a frequency less than 3.5 cycles per second. They often have voltages 2 to 4 times greater than other brain waves and occur in very deep sleep, in infancy and in serious organic brain disease. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 25, 26, 28"},{"q":"Which stage is the deepest sleep of the night?","choices":["Stage N3","Stage N1","Stage N2","Rapid eye movement sleep"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Stage N3, also called deep sleep, is restful sleep and the deepest non-rapid eye movement stage, marked by delta waves. Body and tissue growth and repair occur in it, and it decreases with age. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 9, 28"},{"q":"Which wave runs at 8 to 13 cycles per second, is strongest over the occipital region and appears in healthy adults who are awake and resting quietly?","choices":["Delta waves","Alpha waves","Beta waves","Theta waves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Alpha waves are rhythmical at 8 to 13 cycles per second, are found in almost all healthy adults who are awake and in a quiet, resting state, and occur most intensely in the occipital region. They disappear during deep sleep. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 25, 26"},{"q":"How do rapid eye movement periods change from the first to the last cycles of a night?","choices":["They keep the same length","They are absent until morning","They grow longer","They grow shorter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Rapid eye movement periods lengthen through the night: the first are short and the later ones longer. As a person becomes more rested and less sleepy, the duration of rapid eye movement episodes increases. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 7, 9"},{"q":"Which autonomic change marks non-rapid eye movement sleep?","choices":["Rising blood pressure","Increased parasympathetic tone","Increased peripheral vascular tone","Irregular heart and breathing rates"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Non-rapid eye movement sleep brings increased parasympathetic tone, with low blood pressure, a slow heart rate and a decreased breathing rate. Blood pressure, heart rate, breathing rate and metabolic rate fall by 10 to 30%. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 10, 12, 14"},{"q":"Which circumstance makes rapid eye movement episodes short or even absent?","choices":["Extreme sleepiness","Being well rested","Young adulthood","The late cycles of the night"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When a person is extremely sleepy, each rapid eye movement episode is short or even absent. As the person becomes more restful and less sleepy, the duration of rapid eye movement episodes increases, so the later periods of a night are longer. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 7, 9"},{"q":"At what frequency do alpha waves occur?","choices":["Less than 3.5 cycles per second","4 to 7 cycles per second","8 to 13 cycles per second","Greater than 14 cycles per second"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Alpha waves are rhythmical waves at 8 to 13 cycles per second, found in almost all healthy adults who are awake and in a quiet, resting state, and they are strongest over the occipital region. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 25, 26"},{"q":"Which stage of sleep decreases with age?","choices":["Stage N1","Stage N2","Rapid eye movement sleep","Stage N3"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Stage N3, the deepest, restful non-rapid eye movement stage, marked by delta waves and the time of body and tissue growth and repair, decreases with age. Stage N2 remains the longest stage by total duration, and rapid eye movement sleep is about 25% of a young adult's sleep. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 9, 13, 28"},{"q":"At what frequency do beta waves occur?","choices":["Less than 3.5 cycles per second","4 to 7 cycles per second","8 to 13 cycles per second","Greater than 14 cycles per second"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beta waves occur at frequencies greater than 14 cycles per second, as high as 80, and are asynchronous and low in voltage, recorded mainly from the parietal and frontal regions in an alert, actively processing person. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 24, 25, 26"},{"q":"Which wave persists in the cortex of animals whose cerebral cortex has been separated from the thalamus?","choices":["Beta waves","Theta waves","Delta waves","Alpha waves"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Delta waves strictly occur in the cortex, independent of activities in lower regions of the brain, so they appear in the cortex of animals with subcortical transections that separate the cerebral cortex from the thalamus. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 25, 26"}]};
